adjudication.rationale:
In `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the handler calls `symmetricDecrypt(...)` and immediately `JSON.parse(...)` the result:
```ts
const keys = JSON.parse(
  symmetricDecrypt(reqBody.keys, process.env.CALCOM_APP_CREDENTIAL_ENCRYPTION_KEY || "")
);
```
There is no surrounding `try/catch` (nor other error handling) for decryption failures, invalid ciphertext, missing/empty encryption key, or invalid JSON. Any of these will throw and bubble up as an unhandled exception, producing a generic 500 and potentially exposing stack traces depending on runtime configuration (e.g., dev mode / error handling middleware).

The new endpoint `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts` authenticates requests by comparing a header value to `process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET` using `!==`:

```ts
if (req.headers[... ] !== process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET) {
  return res.status(403).json({ message: "Invalid webhook secret" });
}
```

This is a direct string comparison that is not constant-time, and it is applied to a secret used to authorize writes to credentials. A constant-time comparison (e.g., `crypto.timingSafeEqual` with normalized Buffer lengths) is the standard mitigation for timing side-channels on secret equality checks, so the finding points to a real security weakness introduced by the diff.

Yes. In `packages/app-store/_utils/oauth/parseRefreshTokenResponse.ts`, after parsing the refresh response, the function does:

```ts
if (!refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token) {
  refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token = "refresh_token";
}
```

This concretely overwrites a missing `refresh_token` with the literal string `"refresh_token"`. In the diff, `Office365CalendarService` uses this helper on the real token refresh response (`responseJson`) and then merges `tokenResponse.data` back into stored credentials:

```ts
const tokenResponse = parseRefreshTokenResponse(responseJson, refreshTokenResponseSchema);
o365AuthCredentials = { ...o365AuthCredentials, ...(tokenResponse.success && tokenResponse.data) };
```

So when an OAuth provider does not return `refresh_token` on refresh (a common case), the stored credential will be updated to `refresh_token: "refresh_token"`, which will cause the next refresh cycle to fail ("brick" the credential).

In the new API route `apps/web/pages/api/webhook/app-credential.ts`, the webhook authentication check does a direct string inequality comparison:

```ts
if (
  req.headers[process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_HEADER_NAME || "calcom-webhook-secret"] !==
  process.env.CALCOM_WEBHOOK_SECRET
) {
  return res.status(403).json({ message: "Invalid webhook secret" });
}
```

This uses normal JS string comparison (`!==` / `===` semantics), not a constant-time comparison (e.g., `crypto.timingSafeEqual`). That creates a real timing side-channel surface for guessing the secret, at least in principle, and is a recognizable security issue introduced by the diff.

The diff introduces `parseRefreshTokenResponse`, which explicitly does:

```ts
if (!refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token) {
  refreshTokenResponse.data.refresh_token = "refresh_token";
}
```

When credential sharing is enabled (`APP_CREDENTIAL_SHARING_ENABLED` + `CALCOM_CREDENTIAL_SYNC_ENDPOINT`), it validates responses against `minimumTokenResponseSchema`, which does not require `refresh_token`. That means a token refresh response lacking `refresh_token` will be accepted and then have `refresh_token` forcibly set to the literal string.

This can corrupt persisted credentials. In `Office365CalendarService`, the returned `.data` is merged into the existing credentials:

```ts
o365AuthCredentials = { ...o365AuthCredentials, ...(tokenResponse.success && tokenResponse.data) };
await prisma.credential.update({ data: { key: o365AuthCredentials } });
```

So if the response didn’t include a refresh token, the existing valid `o365AuthCredentials.refresh_token` will be overwritten with the literal `"refresh_token"` and saved to the DB. If the system later attempts to use the stored refresh token (e.g., sharing disabled, endpoint misconfigured, or code path changes), refresh will fail because the stored refresh token is invalid.

Therefore the finding describes a real defect introduced in this diff.
